Hard-coded = a value is directly written into the source code and must be manually changed when the value changes.

In DevOps/MLOps, we generally try to avoid hard-coding configuration because it makes deployment and maintenance harder.

===

Hard-coded means writing a value directly inside your code instead of getting it from a configuration, environment variable, or another external source.

Simple example

Suppose you are deploying your Flask application to EC2.

Hard-coded:

aws_region = "us-east-1"
bucket_name = "my-insurance-bucket"

Here, the values are directly written in the code.

If the bucket changes, you have to edit the code.

====
Better approach

Store the values outside the code:

import os

aws_region = os.getenv("AWS_REGION")
bucket_name = os.getenv("BUCKET_NAME")

Then provide the values through environment/configuration:

AWS_REGION = us-east-1
BUCKET_NAME = my-insurance-bucket

Now you can change the bucket or region without changing your Python code.

In your drawing

You wrote:

Insurance → Flask → with hard coded → EC2 → hosting

This likely means your original Flask application has things like:

S3 bucket name
AWS region
API URLs
file paths
credentials/configuration

directly written in the Flask code.

For example:

s3_bucket = "rsugumar-insurance-prediction-ml-project-2026-01"

That is hard-coded.

⚠️ One important thing

Never hard-code AWS Access Key ID or Secret Access Key in your Flask code.

❌ Bad:

AWS_ACCESS_KEY = "AKIA..."
AWS_SECRET_KEY = "xxxxxxxx"

Instead use IAM roles when running on EC2, or environment variables/secrets where appropriate.

